 "explanation": "1.6.8.0's form (patch/full_backup_1680.json) with one change in ALIGN, in the same 7 words: every other routine keeps its address and its words (VDUMP_BYTE, ALIGN_REFRESH, BACKUP_SEND, BACKUP_CHECK, BACKUP, BK_SKIP, SETTINGS_SEND, the 5 hook words). Why: 1.6.8.0 re-aligned a chip by 0xF5nn only when its start-up word was exactly 0x16 (Tempester Voice 1.5.1.0 and 1.5.1.1); Tempester Voice 1.5.1.2 answers 0x17, and every Tempester Voice from 1.5.1.0 on clears its dump pointer and byte phase on 0xF5nn (patch/voice/parser_guard.json). ALIGN now re-aligns a chip whose 0xFE00 halfword w is 0x16 <= w <= 0x79 (unsigned: w - 0x16 < 0x64), the bounds of n°63's 1.6.9.0 form (patch/release_cap_1690.json): DSI 0x15 and a chip that did not answer (0x0000, 0xFFFF) are refused (never read forward, read once per power-on, empty 78+k after), 0x79 is the last word main's start-up loop takes. The word's address is taken from gp ([address] + 2 chip = gp + 0x5648 + 2 chip), which frees the instruction the range test needs. Invariant unchanged: no chip is asked for a stream byte at or above 66,048. Reads only, plus 0xF5nn to a Tempester Voice chip (its own start-up number); no flash write. RAM as 1.6.8.0: [address] CHIP, [address] POS[6]. Proof: tools/sim_full_backup.py --range (chips on 1.5.1.0, 1.5.1.1, 1.5.1.2, DSI and words at the bounds) and its counter-proofs (the exact 0x16 test of 1.6.8.0 against 0x17 chips, a range that takes 0x15 or 0x7A). Source: full_backup_1690.s.",
